### Findings

- **F03-1** [major] `crates/c3/src/finish/secret_gate.rs:105` - The secret gate never scans staged typechange (T) entries: `staged_text_paths` filters `--diff-filter=ACMR`, so a tracked symlink replaced by a regular file commits with its new content unscanned (it still appears in the printed staged list, which uses no filter). Trigger: A path is committed as a symlink (or vice versa), then replaced by the other type with secret-shaped content and staged by `c3 finish`'s `git add -A`. Evidence: read-code: The name list is built with `diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR -z`; T is not in the filter set.; read-code: `staged_name_status` (the printed list) runs with no diff-filter, so a T entry is shown to the user as staged yet was never gated. Verify: In a scratch repo, commit a symlink, replace it with a regular file containing `sk-<39 chars>`, `git add -A`, and run the gate; confirm the entry is status T and no SecretHit is returned. Remedy: Add `T` to `--diff-filter` (ACMRT) — a typechange's new side is new content and must be scanned like any addition.
- **F03-2** [major] `crates/c3/src/finish/secret_gate.rs:115` - The gate fail-opens on a failure to LIST staged files (distinct from the already-known per-file diff fail-open): a non-success `git diff --cached --name-only` yields an empty path list, `scan_staged` returns no hits, and the commit proceeds as clean. Trigger: The `git diff --cached --name-only ... -z` subprocess fails (broken index, lock contention, git error) during `c3 finish`. Evidence: read-code: `match out { Ok(o) if o.status.success() => ...collect(), _ => Vec::new() }` — the error arm silently produces an empty list.; read-code: `scan_staged`'s empty result is treated as clean and the flow continues to commit. Verify: Make the listing command fail (e.g. GIT_INDEX_FILE pointing at a corrupt index) in a test harness and confirm `c3 finish` commits instead of refusing. Remedy: Fail closed: propagate the listing error as `FinishError::Other` instead of returning an empty vector.
- **F03-3** [major] `crates/c3/src/engines/tree_check.rs:187` - `agy_tool_is_writing` whitelists by substring: any tool whose name merely CONTAINS read/view/list/grep/glob/search/find/cat/ls is classified read-only, so writing tools named e.g. `search_replace`, `find_replace`, `read_write_file` or `catalog_put` (contains "cat") pass the drift check and the run only WARNs. Trigger: An agy reviewer calls a writing tool whose name embeds one of the nine read-only substrings while the working tree/collab changes; the tree check records `warned` and the reply stays usable. Evidence: read-code: `!READ_ONLY.iter().any(|ro| t.contains(ro))` — containment, not equality or prefix, decides read-only.; read-code: `agy_warns` requires only that no tool name is classified writing; the classifier is the sole guard. Verify: Call `agy_tool_is_writing("search_replace")` and confirm it returns false; then grep agy fixtures/docs for real tool names containing a read-only substring. Remedy: Match whole names or documented prefixes (split on `_`/`-` and compare tokens), keeping empty/unknown as writing.
- **F03-4** [major] `crates/c3/src/engines/tree_check.rs:163`, `crates/c3/src/engines/tree_check.rs:145` - The collab-scope relaxation lets a reviewer that DID write pass as usable whenever the write lands under an ignored path: another task's directory (`<other>/...`), any `.consult.*`/`..consult.*`-named path at any depth, a sibling handoff prefix during panels, and this task's coordinator-owned `state.md`/`findings.json`/`sessions.json` plus their atomic-write temps — the latter being the ledger the coordinator consumes. Trigger: A misbehaving reviewer writes to `<task>/findings.json` (or another task's directory, or a path it names `.consult.<anything>`) during its run; `compare_collab` drops the change and the outcome is `warned`/`clean`. Evidence: read-code: `belongs_to_other_task`, `is_control_file` (basename prefix match at any depth) and `task_owned_ignores` all remove paths from the comparison before the change is counted.; read-code: Panel members additionally ignore all sibling `handoffs/NN-` prefixes. Verify: In a unit test, add `<task>/findings.json` to the after-snapshot with different content and confirm `compare_collab` returns it as unchanged. Remedy: Keep the ignores, but record them in the tree-check outcome (e.g. a `ignored:` count/path list) so a coordinator sees that writes to ledger-owned paths are structurally invisible; or have the ledger carry a hash the coordinator re-verifies after the run.
- **F03-5** [minor] `crates/c3-core/src/retention.rs:140`, `crates/c3-core/src/health.rs:1169` - The retention RULES table (`RULES`, `is_live`, `clears`, `precedence`) is consumed only by its own tests; health.rs/quota.rs call just the scalar predicates and keep their own inline ordering, so the module billed as "the documented source of truth" can drift from runtime behaviour invisibly. Trigger: A future edit changes health.rs's inline condition (e.g. the model-mark clear or quota ordering) without touching RULES; every test still passes and the table now documents a policy the code does not implement. Evidence: ran-command: The only non-test callers are inside retention.rs itself; health.rs and quota.rs reference only out_minutes/model_unsupported_live/within_*/window_* helpers.; read-code: The model-mark, auth and quota liveness/clearing decisions are inline record-order logic, not calls into the table. Verify: Temporarily invert one RULES row (e.g. Auth cleared_by) and run `cargo test -p c3-core`; nothing fails because nothing consumes it. Remedy: Either route the health/quota decisions through `is_live`/`clears`, or add a test asserting each health.rs decision matches the corresponding RULES row.
- **F03-6** [minor] `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:636`, `crates/c3/src/finish/git.rs:1` - `c3 work` writes to git's object database (`git hash-object -w`, including `--stdin` for the empty blob) outside `finish/`, contradicting the stated invariant that finish is "the one code path in C3 that runs a git write"; failures are silently swallowed (`unwrap_or_default`), degrading patches to empty. Trigger: Any `c3 work --role tests` run on a repo where the object write fails (read-only .git, permissions) — baseline hashes become "", pre-dirty files drop out of `files_changed` and `patch.diff` renders empty with no error surfaced. Evidence: read-code: `store_blob`/`empty_blob` run `hash-object -w`; `baseline_hashes` uses `.unwrap_or_default()` on the result.; read-code: The module doc states c3 only ever runs git write commands from finish. Verify: Run a work test with the repo's objects dir made read-only and observe the run completes with an empty patch and no warning. Remedy: Either amend the invariant text ("writes only loose objects outside finish") and log the write failure where it degrades a patch, or route the object writes through finish/git.rs.
- **F03-7** [note] `crates/c3/src/panel/run.rs:1943` - Re-seat eligibility classifies refusals by substring match on C3's own preflight message wording (" is not usable:", " does not offer the model ", "availability could not be established"); a wording change in the preflight verdicts silently disables re-seating (fail-safe: the panel runs short). Trigger: health/verdict refusal text is reworded in a later release; `lost_before_start` returns false for a genuine lost-before-start member. Evidence: read-code: `is_preflight_refusal` matches three literal substrings; no shared constant links it to the producer of those texts. Verify: Grep the preflight verdict producers for the three phrases and confirm they still emit them verbatim. Remedy: Carry a machine-readable refusal class on the preflight verdict instead of re-matching prose.
- **F03-8** [note] `crates/c3/src/finish/secret_gate.rs:51` - A staged text file whose first 8 KiB contain a NUL byte is skipped as binary, so a secret placed after binary-looking padding bypasses the gate even though the file is committed as text. Trigger: A file with an early NUL and a secret-shaped line after the first 8 KiB is staged and committed. Evidence: read-code: `is_binary` checks only the first 8192 bytes; `scan_staged_text` returns None for such content by design. Verify: Stage a file of NUL padding followed by an `sk-...` line and confirm `scan_staged_text` returns None. Remedy: For the gate (not discovery), scan the extracted added lines for NUL-free windows — or scan the whole added text when it has no NUL in the added lines themselves.

