## Q1

`c3 finish` resolves the git toplevel (`providers.rs:2300`), runs `git add -A` (`finish/git.rs:33`), unstages `NEVER_COMMIT` (`never_commit.rs:14-22`, `finish/mod.rs:184-187`), and scans the `+` lines of `git diff --cached -U0` (`secret_gate.rs:60-72`, `82-93`).

It can commit machine-local artifacts the globs miss. `.gitignore` ignores only the consult lock and pending files (`.gitignore:10-12`). A work resume keeps `.events-resume-<n>.jsonl` and `.stderr-resume-<n>` when the append fails (`work/mod.rs:1490-1540`). Those names match neither `events.jsonl`, `*.events.jsonl`, nor a file whose name is exactly `.stderr`. Detached `.consult.detached-<id8>.prompt.txt` and `.status.json` (`detached.rs:289-291`) are outside the set as well, so `git add -A` commits them.

Finish cannot stage a path outside the work tree. There is no pathspec, and the index belongs to the toplevel `resolve_repo_root` found.

Added-lines-only is a sound trade-off for an unchanged fixture line: a line this commit changes is a `+` line and is scanned. A new blob Git prints as `Binary files differ` has no `+` lines (`secret_gate.rs:119-121`), and the commit message is passed to `git commit -m` unscanned (`finish/git.rs:95-99`). The known fail-open on an unreadable diff and the `+++` line drop are left to the fix already in progress.

## Q2

Yes. Three separate holes let a reviewer that wrote stay usable.

**Ignored collab paths become `clean`.** `compare_collab` drops another task's directory (`tree_check.rs:118-119`, `145-148`), `.consult.*` (`155-157`), and this task's `state.md`, `findings.json`, `sessions.json` and their temps (`163-170`). When those are the only writes, `why` is empty and `engine_tree_outcome` returns `clean` before it looks at tools (`tree.rs:184-190`). The agy writing-tool test is below that return (`192-198`). An agy turn that calls `write_to_file` and writes only `findings.json`, a `.consult.*` file, or another task's tree stays usable with outcome `clean`.

**Claude warns on a real write.** `engine_write_disabled` includes `claude` with no further check (`tree.rs:61`). A watched change becomes `warned` and an empty problem (`199-205`). The reply is discarded only when `problem` is non-empty (`consult/orchestrate/mod.rs:4575-4578`). The init proof fails a turn whose init lists tools outside the allowlist (`engines/claude.rs:451-468`) and returns no problem when `init_count == 0` (`442-444`). A hook write, or a turn that never emitted init, keeps a usable reply.

**Agy's classifier and the snapshot window.** A tool name is treated as read-only when it contains `read`, `view`, `list`, `grep`, `glob`, `search`, `find`, `cat`, or `ls` (`tree_check.rs:179-187`). `write_to_file` and `run_command` still count as writing; `search_replace` or `find_and_replace` would not. Names come only from the primary turn (`consult/orchestrate/mod.rs:3720-3726`); an empty `tool_name` is dropped (`engines/agy.rs:437-440`). The snapshot is taken once, before the denial retry and the timeout continuation (`consult/orchestrate/mod.rs:4538-4598`, continuation at `4625`). A write on those later turns is never judged.

## Q3

The new retention table is not what the verdict runs. `health.rs` calls the window helpers. `verdict_with_credential` is still a fixed chain: missing credential, auth, known-reset quota, reset-less quota, then the per-model mark (`verdict.rs:113-208`). `RULES.precedence`, `is_live`, and `clears` (`retention.rs:140-189`, `266-299`) have no caller on that path.

The table says a model-unsupported mark clears only on a later usable reply from the same model. `endpoint_health_set` keeps the mark only when the newest record for that model is itself a live model-unsupported failure (`health.rs:1212-1223`). A newer quota or transport failure for that model drops the mark.

The new parameter structs (`FinishOpts`, `SlotBuild`, `DeathInputs`, `RunDeathInputs`) use named fields, so a positional swap is not available. I could not diff them against `0b712c4`.

## Q4

The seed still draws the original seats. `panel_seed` is task, purpose, brief sha, lineages, and nonce (`panel/plan.rs:688`). The reserve continues that seed at slot `seats.len()+1` (`panel/routing.rs:623-624`) and excludes seated roster positions (`615-618`). Re-seat does not re-roll slots 1..k. `routing.picked` is that original draw (`panel/plan.rs:825-834`) and is copied into every member spec, including the substitute (`panel/run.rs:2125`). It is not rewritten after a promotion, so the persisted draw still names the lost member.

The substitute is a different roster position. It reuses the lost seat's `k` (`panel/run.rs:1223-1234`) and takes the next `n`/`nn` (`1226-1228`; initial numbers at `942-944`). Ledger lookup is by that same `k` (`1822`), so a later entry shadows an earlier one for the seat.

The published result still describes the lost seat. `render_summary` reads `slots[row.runner]`, the original slot (`1558-1572`). A before-start loss has no usable entry, so `all_usable` becomes false (`1631-1645`) and the exit is 1 (`1690`). The substitute remains on the "not picked" line (`1680-1687`).

The substitute also bypasses the endpoint cap. Reserve runners get a private group (`panel/run.rs:713-742`). The parallel check counts only that group (`1288-1295`, `1306-1326`), so a reserve on an endpoint that already has a running seat is not held to that seat's limit. The panel-wide `--panel-concurrency` cap still counts every running slot (`1285`).

## Q5

The consult reader can attach a death record while the bridge is alive. `ensure_reader_death_record` re-reads, computes `bridge_alive`, and writes `death` either way (`consult/detach.rs:1218-1242`). The caller enters from the earlier judgement (`1584`). `pid_alive` is false when `process_start_iso` returns `None` (`liveness/proc.rs:149-155`). On Windows that includes any non-zero exit `FILETIME` (`1371-1396`). `GetProcessTimes` documents that exit time as undefined for a process that has not exited. The background keeps the status record in memory and writes it back without re-reading (`consult/detach.rs:120-127`), so the two writers overwrite each other. `write_report` prints a death block whenever `death` is set (`1805`), including when the fresh judgement is `running`.

A dead bridge with a live engine child is judged `died` from the bridge pid alone (`consult/detached.rs:481-508`). `engine_alive: Some(true)` only sets `vanished` to `bridge` (`208-210`).

Work's `recover_dead_run` re-checks under the admission lock and writes `died` only when the same generation is still pending and not live (`work/record.rs:219-241`).

## Q6

`c3 work` writes dirty working-tree bytes into the object store with `git hash-object -w` (`work/record.rs:577-636`) and stores the diff in `patch.diff`, which is not in `NEVER_COMMIT`. Those blobs are not a commit, and a later `c3 finish` does not scan unreachable objects. I would hold 0.2.4 for the Q2 `clean` hole and the Q4 exit and parallel-limit bugs. The signed-priors pin of `xelth-2026-1` (`router/sign.rs:23-26`) fails closed on an unknown key.

States touched: finish is stage → unstage → scan → commit, and a scan hit returns before commit with the index still staged. The drift check is one snapshot pair after the primary turn; `clean` / `warned` / `failed`, and only `failed` clears `usable`. A panel seat is reserved → running → done, and a non-required before-start preflight loss (`run.rs:1935-1937`) pushes one reserve slot. A detached run is starting → running → done, or `died` when the recorded pid fails `pid_alive`.

