- **F05-8** [major] `crates/c3/src/consult/detach.rs:1221`, `crates/c3/src/liveness/proc.rs:1394`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detach.rs:120`, `crates/c3/src/consult/detached.rs:501` - The consult status reader writes a death record even when the fresh pid check says the bridge is alive, and on Windows pid_alive is false for any process whose GetProcessTimes exit FILETIME is non-zero. The background then overwrites the status file from an in-memory copy. A dead bridge with a live engine child is judged died from the bridge pid alone. Trigger: c3 consult --status (or --wait) while a detached background is still running and process_start_iso returns None, or after the bridge process has exited while its engine child still runs. Evidence: read-code: ensure_reader_death_record computes bridge_alive and always assigns fresh.death unless state is done or death is already set. The caller enters when the earlier judgement was died.; read-code: pid_alive is false when process_start_iso returns None. The Windows implementation returns None when the exit FILETIME is non-zero.; inferred: The API contract says the exit time is undefined if the process has not exited. A non-zero value on a live process makes pid_alive false.; read-code: update_member writes Sink.record without re-reading. write_report prints death whenever it is set. judgement returns died when pid_alive is false, and engine_alive does not change that state. Verify: RC2 below. Also start a detached run, and from another process call the reader path while the bridge pid is alive; the status file must not gain a death object. Remedy: Write the death record only when the fresh read still says the bridge is dead, under the same lock as the background's status write. Have the background re-read before write. Treat a non-zero exit FILETIME as gone only together with a wait that shows the process has actually exited. Keep state running while engine_alive is true.
- **F05-9** [minor] `crates/c3-core/src/retention.rs:172`, `crates/c3-core/src/health.rs:1212`, `crates/c3-core/src/verdict.rs:113` - The retention table says a model-unsupported mark clears only on a later usable reply from the same model. endpoint_health_set drops the mark whenever the newest record for that model is not itself a model-unsupported failure, including a later quota or transport failure. The verdict if-else does not read RULES.precedence, is_live, or clears. Trigger: A model-unsupported failure for model M, then a later quota failure for the same endpoint and model M, still inside 24 hours. Evidence: read-code: MarkKind::ModelUnsupported is ClearedBy::UsableReplyOfModel, implemented as usable_reply && same_model.; read-code: The newest record per model is considered once. The mark is pushed only when that record is a non-ok model-unsupported failure inside the window.; read-code: verdict_with_credential branches on credential, auth, quota, then models_unsupported. It does not call retention::clears or retention::rule. Verify: Feed endpoint_health_set two records for one model: an older model-unsupported failure and a newer quota failure inside 24h. Confirm models_unsupported is empty. Remedy: Make endpoint_health_set call clears, so only a newer usable reply from that model removes the mark, or change the table row to say any newer record for the model wins.
- **F05-10** [note] `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:577`, `crates/c3/src/work/record.rs:635` - c3 work writes dirty working-tree file bytes into the git object store with git hash-object -w. Those blobs are not a commit and c3 finish's secret gate does not scan unreachable objects. patch.diff from the same bytes is not in NEVER_COMMIT. Trigger: A work run whose baseline or result includes a dirty file that holds a secret, then c3 finish. Evidence: read-code: baseline_hashes and file_patch call store_blob, which runs git hash-object -w on the working-tree path. The comment says the object is loose and unreferenced. Verify: Dirty a file with an sk- key, run a work record that calls baseline_hashes, and confirm git cat-file -t <printed-id> is blob while git rev-list --all --objects does not name it. Remedy: Hash with git hash-object without -w, or write the blob only under the work run directory, and add patch.diff to the never-commit set.

